@page "/quick-start"
@using FlexCore.Showcase.Services

@* 2026-11-04: Quick Start = download of the home page window's app as a Blazor Server project. The zip is built from
   quickstart/RazorSharpGetaways on every build (FlexCore.Showcase.csproj) and served from the assembly
   (Services/QuickStartDownload.cs), so it is always the home page's code. Get Started covers existing apps. *@

<PageTitle>FlexCore Quick Start: a Blazor App to Download and Run</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-page">
    <h1>Quick Start</h1>
    <p>
        Razor Sharp Getaways is a complete Blazor Server app built on FlexCore controls: five pages, in-memory data, nothing to
        configure. It is the app in the window on the <a href="./">home page</a>. Download it, run it, change it.
    </p>

    <p class="qs-download">
        <a class="qs-button" href="@QuickStartDownload.Path" download><span class="bi bi-download"></span> Download Razor Sharp Getaways (zip)</a>
        <span class="qs-note">Blazor Server, .NET 10, FlexCore from NuGet.</span>
    </p>

    <section>
        <h2>Run it</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Unzip the download; the project is in the <code>RazorSharpGetaways</code> folder.</li>
            <li>Install the <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet/10.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">.NET 10 SDK</a> if the machine does not have it.</li>
            <li>Run the project from that folder and open the address it prints. The first run restores the FlexCore package.
                <pre><code>dotnet run</code></pre>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            If the browser warns about the certificate, run <code>dotnet dev-certs https --trust</code> once, or start the app with
            <code>dotnet run --launch-profile http</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>What is in it</h2>
        <table class="qs-pages">
            <thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>What it shows</th><th>Main FlexCore controls</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Four figures, trip requests by month and upcoming departures; press Rearrange the cards to drag them into a new order</td><td>GridLayoutControl, CardControl, ChartControl, GridControl, SegmentedControl, CheckBoxControl</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Trip Requests</td><td>1,000 requests with a Quote cell you type straight into, an in-cell lookup, filter chips, search and a quote editor</td><td>GridControl, DropDownGridControl, ChipListControl, SearchBoxControl, ToolbarControl, StatusBarControl, DialogControl, DatePickerControl, NumericTextBoxControl</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Itinerary</td><td>One booked trip, from the flight out to the flight home</td><td>SchedulerControl</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Invoices</td><td>A booked trip's package cost sheet: a report definition in the Crystal Reports XML export format, with rows built in memory</td><td>ReportWriterControl</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Settings</td><td>Colour scheme, text size and grid look with a live preview, plus the dashboard layout</td><td>ColorPaletteControl, SliderControl, SwitchControl, SegmentedControl, DropDownListControl</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            The shell is a vertical <code>TabStripControl</code> menu, an <code>AppBarControl</code> with <code>ButtonControl</code> menus and
            an <code>AvatarControl</code>, and a <code>FabMenuControl</code>. The folders are the ones <code>dotnet new blazor</code> creates,
            plus <code>Components/Getaways</code> for the page components and the shell, <code>Data</code> for the in-memory store, the
            settings and the report rows, <code>wwwroot/reports</code> for the report and <code>wwwroot/getaways.css</code> for the styles.
            The README says where each part lives and how FlexCore is wired in; the service registrations are in <code>Program.cs</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>The same code as the home page</h2>
        <p>
            The home page compiles the project's <code>Components/Getaways</code> and <code>Data</code> files into its demo frame, and its
            Razor and C# tabs print each page's markup and <code>&#64;code</code> from them. The download adds its own <code>Program.cs</code>,
            layout and routed pages, and takes FlexCore from NuGet where this site builds against the library's source.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Next</h2>
        <p>
            Follow <a href="get-started">Get Started</a> to wire FlexCore into an app of your own: package, namespaces, services, hosts,
            interactivity, first grid. Open <a href="components">All components</a> for the controls in 13 groups; each row opens a live
            demo with its code. Read the <a href="faq">FAQ</a> for compatibility, capabilities, support and comparisons.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
